Exactly. You can change what a memory gives you by changing the cue you use to reopen it. “Same tune, different fate” can bring back the sphere and torus. Open it with storytelling in mind and you get two travelers following identical directions through different worlds. Open it with version control in mind and you get the same change applied to two branches with different histories.

The part that compounds is saving the new connection beside the original seed. Next time, you can enter through the math, the story, or the code. Any of those cues can bring the other two back into view. You’ve made the same idea easier to find and given yourself more to do with it.

And the hack cuts both ways. A future model might tell the travelers’ story so vividly that it feels like it was in your original sketch. A few words can preserve the lineage: “The loop was original. The travelers came later.” That lets Pantheon keep the surprise and remember where it came from.